\title[Normalized spectral transforms of the $an+1$ maps]
      {Normalized Spectral Transforms of the $an+1$ Maps:\\
       An Exact Limit Law, Its Resonance Arithmetic,\\ and a No-Go Theorem}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For an integer multiplier $a\ge 1$ let $T_a$ denote the accelerated one-step map
$T_a(n)=n/2$ for even $n$ and $T_a(n)=an+1$ for odd $n$, and let $r_a(n)=T_a(n)/n$
be the associated \emph{phase ratio}.  We study the cutoff exponential sum
$F_a(\omega,N)=\sum_{n\le N}e(\omega r_a(n))$, where $e(x)=e^{2\pi i x}$.
We prove the exact limit law $F_a(\omega,N)/N\to A_a(\omega)=\bigl(e(\omega/2)+e(a\omega)\bigr)/2$
with the explicit, multiplier-uniform error bound
$\bigl|F_a(\omega,N)/N-A_a(\omega)\bigr|\le\bigl(1+2\pi|\omega|(1+\log N)\bigr)/N$
for all $N\ge1$; consequently the convergence is uniform on compact frequency sets,
simultaneously for all multipliers.  The modulus of the amplitude is the single cosine
$|A_a(\omega)|=|\cos(\pi(a-\tfrac12)\omega)|$, so the resonance set on which genuine
$o(N)$ cancellation occurs is exactly $R_a=\{\omega:(2a-1)\omega\in2\mathbb{Z}+1\}$.
Off $R_a$ the transform has full linear size, and near $\omega=0$ one has
$|F_a(\omega,N)|\ge N/4$ eventually, refuting any pointwise decay statement over all
irrational frequencies.  All multipliers share the trivial resonances at odd integers
and have amplitude $1$ at even integers, but the classical maps $3n+1$, $5n+1$, $7n+1$
are pairwise separated at $\omega=1/5,\,1/9,\,1/13$, and pairwise resonate together only
at odd integers.  The mean square $\int_0^2|A_a(\omega)|^2\,d\omega=1$ for every $a$, so
$L^2$-averaging cannot discriminate.  Finally we prove a no-go theorem: if two phase-ratio
functions differ only on a set of density zero, their normalized transforms have the same
limit; in particular finite surgery on the map (inserting or destroying a cycle) leaves the
normalized spectrum unchanged, so no implication ``spectral estimate $\Rightarrow$ orbit
hitting-time estimate'' can hold for the one-step cutoff sum.  We close with two precise
conjectures identifying where dynamical information can still survive.
\end{abstract}

